{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, while behaviour without the flag stays exactly as it is today.\n\nContext (current repo state):\n- bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and writes `JSON.stringify(parse(text))` plus a newline. It ignores process.argv.\n- src/parse.js exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`, which splits rows on /\\r?\\n/ and fields on sep.\n- test/csv.test.js uses node:test and has a 'cli' test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js` via execFileSync with stdin input.\n- package.json: \"test\": \"node --test\".\n- README.md shows only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether '--tsv' appears anywhere in process.argv.slice(2). If it does, call parse(text, '\\t'); otherwise call parse(text) exactly as today. Keep reading from stdin only, and keep the output format the same (JSON.stringify with no indentation, then a single '\\n'). Leave src/parse.js unchanged unless you really need to change it, and leave the comma default unchanged.\n2. Do not add quoting rules, separator auto-detection, a general --sep/--delimiter option, --help, or handling of unknown arguments. Those are out of scope.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, or in a new file under test/, add node:test tests that run bin/csv2json.js through execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }) and assert the exact output for:\n   - 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'\n   - 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' -> '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n'\n   - 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' -> '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n'\n   Also add a test without the flag on 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' that expects the current output, which is the JSON text [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}] plus a newline (in a JS string literal: '[{\"a\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\t2\"}]\\n'). Keep the existing tests.\n4. In README.md, document the flag with an example invocation such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`, and say that --tsv reads tab-separated input. Keep the existing CSV example.\n5. From the repo root, check by hand:\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"  -> [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] then exit=0\n   - printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  -> [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  -> [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\n   - printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js  -> [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\n   - printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js  -> [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}] (with a literal backslash-t JSON escape)\n   - npm test exits 0.\n6. Commit the changes to bin/csv2json.js, the test file(s) and README.md, and open one pull request that references https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run everything from the repository root. Do one check per expect line, in this order. Record PASS or FAIL for each check with the command output as evidence. Compare stdout exactly. A useful way to see trailing newlines and tabs is to pipe through `od -c`, or to capture with `out=$(...; printf x)`.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c; then run printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\".\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] followed by one newline, and the exit status is 0. FAIL: any other stdout, or a non-zero exit.\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}] followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv | od -c\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}] followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: \"Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\"\nRun: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c\nPASS: stdout is exactly [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: \"Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\"\nRun: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js | od -c\nPASS: stdout is exactly the JSON text [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}], where each \\t is the two characters backslash and t (the JSON tab escape), followed by a newline. FAIL: anything else, for example output split into keys a and b.\n\nCheck 6. Expect: \"`npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\"\nRun: npm test; echo \"exit=$?\". Then run grep -rn -- '--tsv' test/ and read the matching test code.\nPASS: npm test exits 0, and at least one test under test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the --tsv argument on input that contains tab characters and asserts on the output it produces. FAIL: npm test exits non-zero, or no such test exists (for example the only --tsv test calls parse() directly instead of running bin/csv2json.js, uses no tab input, or makes no assertion on the output).\n\nCheck 7. Expect: \"README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\"\nRun: cat README.md\nPASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag and includes an example command that uses it to run the CLI (for example node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv). FAIL: the flag is not mentioned, or there is no example invocation that uses it.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- The issue's proof notes mention `npm run test:cli`, but package.json has no such script. Use `npm test` for check 6.\n- Optional extra checks: --tsv placed among other arguments still works; src/parse.js keeps its ',' default; the diff stays within scope (no --sep, no quoting, no --help)."}